1
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:11,033
A system's backtest. Five years of data, and the profit curve goes straight up.

2
00:00:11,484 --> 00:00:14,879
Then it went live, and lost money in the very first month.

3
00:00:15,330 --> 00:00:19,927
The system was not the problem. The backtest was done the wrong way.

4
00:00:20,927 --> 00:00:25,839
Hello, this is episode five of the SPIDER Quant Trading series.

5
00:00:26,289 --> 00:00:30,886
Last time: position sizing, how much to risk per trade.

6
00:00:31,336 --> 00:00:40,244
Today: backtesting. How to test a system before risking money, and which mistakes make the result a lie.

7
00:00:41,244 --> 00:00:49,055
A backtest means running your rules on past data, as if the system had really traded back then.

8
00:00:49,505 --> 00:00:56,114
Record every trade's entry, exit and result, then look at the full picture.

9
00:00:56,564 --> 00:01:01,475
The goal is one question: does this system really have an edge?

10
00:01:02,475 --> 00:01:06,341
First condition: the rules must be written down and clear.

11
00:01:06,791 --> 00:01:13,713
When to enter, where the stop is, where the target is, what size. One answer for each.

12
00:01:14,163 --> 00:01:20,198
If any rule says "decide by looking at the chart", that is a guess, not a backtest.

13
00:01:21,198 --> 00:01:24,750
Second: how much data, and what kind.

14
00:01:25,199 --> 00:01:32,279
Twenty or thirty trades tell you nothing, they could be luck. You need at least a few hundred.

15
00:01:32,729 --> 00:01:39,573
And the data should include different market moods: trends, sideways, and very volatile days.

16
00:01:40,573 --> 00:01:44,440
The third mistake is the most common: forgetting costs.

17
00:01:44,890 --> 00:01:48,860
Every trade pays spread, commission and slippage.

18
00:01:49,309 --> 00:01:57,015
Say 200 trades, averaging 0.15R each. That is 30R in total.

19
00:01:57,466 --> 00:02:05,015
If each trade costs 0.1R, 20R goes to costs. Only 10R is left.

20
00:02:06,015 --> 00:02:11,005
The fourth mistake is sneaky: using data from the future.

21
00:02:11,455 --> 00:02:18,299
For example, the signal forms at the candle's close, but the backtest enters at that same candle's open.

22
00:02:18,749 --> 00:02:24,704
At the open, nobody knew the close. Live, you could never take that trade like that.

23
00:02:25,155 --> 00:02:30,798
This is called look-ahead bias. It always makes results look better than reality.

24
00:02:31,798 --> 00:02:34,227
The fifth mistake: overfitting.

25
00:02:34,677 --> 00:02:40,790
You test a thousand setting combinations and pick the one that looks best.

26
00:02:41,239 --> 00:02:46,072
That system has memorised the past data. It has not learned the market.

27
00:02:46,522 --> 00:02:50,153
On new data, those same settings often fail.

28
00:02:51,153 --> 00:02:54,863
The fix: split the data into two parts.

29
00:02:55,313 --> 00:03:01,033
On the first part, say 70%, build the system and fix the settings.

30
00:03:01,483 --> 00:03:07,439
Do not touch the other 30%. Test on it only once, at the end.

31
00:03:07,888 --> 00:03:14,001
If it holds up there, confidence grows. If it breaks, go back to the start.

32
00:03:15,001 --> 00:03:18,632
Do not judge a backtest by total profit alone.

33
00:03:19,083 --> 00:03:25,849
Look at the number of trades, the average per trade, called expectancy, and the profit factor.

34
00:03:26,299 --> 00:03:33,456
Profit factor is gross profit divided by gross loss. It must be above one, after costs.

35
00:03:33,906 --> 00:03:40,176
And the biggest drawdown and the longest losing streak. Could you live through them?

36
00:03:41,176 --> 00:03:47,053
A bitter truth: live results are usually weaker than the backtest.

37
00:03:47,503 --> 00:03:52,911
Spreads change, slippage grows, and the market itself changes.

38
00:03:53,361 --> 00:04:01,067
So after a backtest, run it on demo or small size for a few weeks first. That is called a forward test.

39
00:04:02,067 --> 00:04:03,555
So, today's summary.

40
00:04:04,006 --> 00:04:08,447
Write the rules down, use enough data, and include costs.

41
00:04:08,897 --> 00:04:15,819
Never use future data, do not over-tune settings, and test once on out-of-sample data.

42
00:04:16,269 --> 00:04:20,317
Then forward test first, and only after that, real money.

43
00:04:21,318 --> 00:04:27,039
Next episode: risk of ruin. How to estimate the chance of blowing the account.

44
00:04:27,489 --> 00:04:32,400
If this was useful, subscribe and save the playlist.

45
